# {{ .Name }}

A Civitai App built with **web components and no UI framework** — the default
`civitai app init` template:

- **`@civitai/sdk`** for the host bridge — `initialize()` waits for the host and
  hands you the slot context, viewer, theme and `app.host`.
- **`@civitai/components`** `<civitai-*>` custom elements for the UI, themed by
  **`@civitai/theme`**'s `--civitai-*` tokens.
- Vite + TypeScript, nothing else.

It mirrors the `civitai-block-starter` starter in
[civitai/civitai-app-starters](https://github.com/civitai/civitai-app-starters),
as a full-page app (see "Page, not slot" below).

Prefer React? `civitai app init "…" --template page-money` scaffolds the React
alternative: Vite + React + TypeScript on `@civitai/blocks-react`, with a
runnable generation (Buzz-spend) sample.

## What this is

A sandboxed static web app served in an iframe by the Civitai host. The
platform owns the build: it runs `npm ci` and then the `buildCommand` from
`block.manifest.json` (`npm run build`), serving the static output from
`outputDir` (`dist/`). You do NOT commit `dist/` — the platform builds it.

**Commit your lockfile.** The platform installs *strictly* from it — `npm ci`,
with no registry re-resolve fallback — so builds are byte-reproducible. Without
a committed `package-lock.json` the build hard-fails. If you prefer pnpm or
yarn, set `"buildCommand"` to that package manager (plus the `"outputDir"` the
manifest schema requires alongside it) and commit *that* lockfile instead:
`"pnpm run build"` needs `pnpm-lock.yaml`, `"yarn run build"` needs `yarn.lock`.
`civitai app validate` catches a lockfile that disagrees with `buildCommand`
before you submit.

## Develop

```bash
npm install
npm run dev:harness   # http://localhost:5186, with a local mock host
```

`npm run dev:harness` runs Vite with a local dev harness around your app. The
harness stands in for the host page: it posts a fake `BLOCK_INIT`, logs your
outbound messages, and echoes token refreshes, so the UI iterates without
civitai.com embedding it. `.env.development` already allow-lists the harness's
origin (`http://localhost:5186`) and `https://civitai.com`, the real host's
origin when `civitai app dev-tunnel` embeds your dev server; the port is pinned
in `vite.config.ts` and is the dev tunnel's default. If the app sits on its
skeleton, the console says it is still waiting for a host — check that the
host's origin is on that list.

`npm run dev` is the same server **without** the harness — nothing sends
`BLOCK_INIT`, so you will see the boot skeleton until a real host frames the
dev URL.

To see your LOCAL code inside the REAL Civitai host (invite-only beta):

```bash
npm run dev:tunnel        # terminal 1: the embeddable dev server on :5186
civitai app dev-tunnel    # terminal 2: tunnels it and prints the /apps/dev/<blockId> URL
```

`vite.config.ts` makes the dev server embeddable for this — it admits the
`.civit.ai` tunnel host, sends `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` (the host frames
the app at a null origin) and a `frame-ancestors https://civitai.com` CSP, and
routes live reload over the tunnel — all from `src/dev-embed.ts`, and all
dev-only: `vite build` is unaffected.

| Command | What it does |
|---|---|
| `npm run dev:harness` | Vite + the local mock host. **Use this for offline iteration.** |
| `npm run dev` | Vite, no harness — for a real host page that iframes your dev URL. |
| `npm run dev:tunnel` | The embeddable dev server `civitai app dev-tunnel` serves inside the real host. |
| `npm test` | Drives the app through the real bridge in happy-dom (`test/block.test.ts`). |
| `npm run typecheck` | TypeScript strict check. |
| `npm run build` | Production bundle in `dist/`. Also validates `block.manifest.json`. |
| `npm run preview` | Serve the production bundle locally. |

## What runs in the iframe

```ts
import '@civitai/components/register'; // the generic <civitai-*> kit (see below)
import { initialize } from '@civitai/sdk';

const app = await initialize();          // resolves on the host's BLOCK_INIT
document.documentElement.dataset.theme = app.theme;
app.onChange(() => { /* theme, context, token … changed — update the view in place */ });
```

```html
<civitai-stack gap="sm">
  <civitai-text as="h2" size="md" weight="bold">Hello</civitai-text>
  <civitai-badge variant="light">signed in</civitai-badge>
</civitai-stack>
```

The whole demo is `src/block.ts`. It builds the view ONCE and updates it in
place from `app.onChange` — which also fires on every token rotation — so never
rebuild the view there, or a host push wipes whatever the viewer typed.
`src/main.ts` calls `startBlock`, which shows an error in place of the skeleton
if the app cannot start; keep that wiring. From the same `app` you also get `app.site`
(the `/api/v1` REST API as the viewer), `app.storage` / `app.sharedStorage`,
`app.requestGrants(scopes)`, and the host's own UI via `app.host`. The
`@civitai/sdk` README documents each.

Every tag, attribute, event and slot of the `<civitai-*>` elements is listed in
`@civitai/components`' `custom-elements.json` — but `@civitai/components/register`
defines only the generic kit. The civitai-specific elements (`<civitai-avatar>`,
`<civitai-media-card>`, `<civitai-rating-badge>`, `<civitai-reaction>`,
`<civitai-tag>`) need `@civitai/components/register-site` instead, and the
SDK-backed `<civitai-sign-in-button>` / `<civitai-workflow-button>` each need
their own `@civitai/components/<tag>/define`. An element nothing defines renders
as an inert tag with no error; `test/elements.test.ts` fails on any element the
app uses that no import defines.

## The host handshake

The host does not reveal a page app until it answers the host's `BLOCK_INIT`
with `BLOCK_READY`. **`@civitai/sdk` sends that for you** — `initialize()`'s
transport answers the first `BLOCK_INIT`, to the origin it came from, and checks
every inbound message's origin against `VITE_BLOCK_ALLOWED_PARENT_ORIGINS`
(injected by the platform build in production). So, unlike the SDK-free
`static` and `page-vite` templates, this app ships **no** `civitai-host.js` —
and must not gain one: two handshakes race, and the loser can leave the SDK
never having seen an init.

## Page, not slot

The starter this template mirrors targets a model-page slot
(`model.sidebar_top`). This app instead declares a full **page**
(`"page"` in `block.manifest.json`) that the Civitai host runs full-viewport at
`https://civitai.com/apps/run/<slug>` once approved — like every other
`civitai app init` template. So:

- The host sends an `app.page` context (the dev harness does too), which
  `src/block.ts` narrows with `isPageSlotContext`. If you add a model-slot
  `targets` entry, narrow its context with `isModelSlotContext`.
- A page fills the host surface rather than sizing to its content, so there is
  no `app.host.autoResize(root)` call. Add one only alongside a model-slot
  target, where the host honours it.
- There is no direct-load "Open on Civitai" fallback, matching the React
  `page-money` template: opened top-level at its own `<slug>.civit.ai` URL, the
  app keeps its boot skeleton and waits for a host.

## Boot skeleton

`block.manifest.json` sets `"bootSkeleton": true` and `index.html` paints a
matching skeleton inside `#root`, styled by an inline `<style>` so it appears
before any script runs. **Ship them together.** The key makes the run host
stand down its own loading UI; declared over an empty `#root` it is *worse* than
not opting in — a blank iframe for the whole load.

The skeleton is also this app's loading state: it stays until `BLOCK_INIT`
lands, and the mount's one `root.replaceChildren(…)` removes it. Nothing else
does, so keep the skeleton *inside* `#root`.

The boot theme is the **host's**, not the OS's. Civitai apps default to dark and
never consult the browser preference: light engages only when the host says the
viewer chose it — via the `#civitai-block=v1&theme=…` URL fragment (an inline
script applies it before first paint), then `BLOCK_INIT`, then live
`THEME_CHANGE` pushes (synced onto `<html>` by `src/block.ts`).

## Validate

```bash
civitai app validate
```

## Submit

```bash
civitai login        # once, to store your API token
civitai app submit
```

`submit` packages the SOURCE tree (manifest + src + build config), excluding
`.git`, `node_modules`, and `dist` — the platform rebuilds from source.

## Store-listing media (`assets/`)

Your store listing **cannot publish without an icon and a cover**, and both are
settable while the app is in review. `assets/` is scaffolded for them and ships
with no images on purpose — see [`assets/README.md`](assets/README.md) for the
size, format and aspect requirements, then:

```bash
civitai app listing set-icon  ./assets/icon.png
civitai app listing set-cover ./assets/cover.png
civitai app listing status
```
